  "query_preview": "..Mid level ridging centered across the forecast area\nis maintaining dry and stable conditions this evening. Warming\naloft is resulting in an inversion layer a couple thousand feet\nabove the northern valley floors, which is limiting ventilation\nand reducing air quality into the moderate category presently. A\nmean ridge will remain across the region through Thanksgiving,\nmaintaining dry and stable conditions with inversions persisting \nacross northern valleys and basins. This may allow for patchy late\nnight/early morning fog in sheltered locations such as the Cache\nValley and near the GSL. A weak shortwave fighting through this \nridge will split as it moves through the Great Basin, having \nlittle if any discernible impact on the forecast area. With this\nridge remaining in place, a gradual warming trend is expected\nacross central and southern Utah through the remainder of the\nweek. \n\nA shortwave trough brushing by to the northeast is forecast to\npush a cold front through the area Friday night into Saturday. EC\nensemble members remain more aggressive digging this wave a \nlittle further west, and bringing a round of valley rain/mountain \nsnow to northern and perhaps portions of central Utah Friday night\ninto early Saturday. GEFS members continue to remain drier so \nsome uncertainty does remain. \n\nConfidence remains fairly high in a transition to a more\nprogressive and active pattern next week, as a series of shortwave\ntroughs move inland and pass through the region. Uncertainty\nremains regarding timing and track of these waves and the\nresultant chances for precipitation, however late Tuesday into\nWednesday looks most likely for widespread precipitation across\nthe forecast area.",
